    "message": {
      "text": "## Harbor v1 — Retention Risk and Bounded Backpressure/Recovery Plan\n\n### Confirmed decisions (user-directed)\n- v1 = PostgreSQL outbox consumers; Go retained; EU-only storage; EUR 900/month ceiling.\n- 6 consumers as initial peak target, **subject to load validation** (not yet validated).\n- No measured costs will be claimed.\n\n### Retention risk — quantified from tool-measured data\n- Tool output: average load produces **~48.3 GiB/day of raw payloads** (excludes indexes, WAL, bloat, replicas).\n- Proposed 30-day raw retention ≈ **1.45 TiB minimum vs. 100 GiB free** — **confirmed infeasible in PostgreSQL**.\n- More urgent: at average rate, 100 GiB buffers only **~2 days** of traffic. One consumer outage or traffic anomaly exhausts disk.\n- **Critical coupling:** the outbox write is in the same transaction as the business change. Disk-full therefore **halts business writes**, not just delivery. WAL retention from a lagging replication slot accelerates fill.\n\n### Recommended bounded backpressure plan (recommendation, not executed)\nBackpressure must be safe because processing is idempotent by `event_id` — rejecting and retrying is always safe.\n\n- **Level 0 — Normal:** 6 consumers, sustained capacity 3,600 events/s vs. 2,400 peak. Alerts on consumer lag and disk headroom.\n- **Level 1 — Lag breach (recommended trigger: lag \u003e 2 min or 100k unprocessed rows):** page on-call; verify all 6 replicas healthy; check for lock contention or slow queries on the outbox table.\n- **Level 2 — Disk \u003c 20% free or sustained lag:** receiver returns **HTTP 429 with `Retry-After`** to webhook senders. Senders retry; idempotency makes this lossless. Business writes continue.\n- **Level 3 — Disk \u003c 10% free:** reject ingest entirely (503), stop lower-priority consumers to free I/O for the business path, and disable any replication slot that is pinning WAL. Hard cap: never let the outbox transaction endanger the business database.\n- **Bounds everywhere:** fixed consumer count cap, explicit lag thresholds, explicit disk thresholds — no unbounded queues.\n\n### Recommended recovery plan\n- **Drain math (from measured throughput):** 6 consumers at 3,600/s vs. 400/s average inflow → net drain ~3,200/s. A 2-hour average-rate outage (~2.9M events, ~4.3 GiB raw) drains in **~15 min**. A full 20-minute peak backlog (2.88M events) drains during a subsequent peak at net 1,200/s → **~40 min**, faster off-peak.\n- **Replay:** after any failed batch, re-drive from the outbox; `event_id` idempotency means no dedup logic is needed.\n- **Data recovery:** the Stage-1 recommendation (raw payloads to EU object storage, metadata + pointers in PostgreSQL) removes the disk-exhaustion failure mode entirely; PostgreSQL then holds only metadata for 90 days — its size is **not yet measured** and needs estimation.\n- **Resilience note:** losing one consumer leaves 5 × 600 = 3,000/s — above the 2,400/s peak but below the 3,600/s headroom target. Recommend validating whether to run 7 replicas or accept reduced headroom during a replica failure.\n\n### Load-validation gates before 15 Nov 2026 launch (not yet done)\n1. Sustain 2,400 events/s for \u003e 20 min on 6 consumers.\n2. Kill one consumer mid-peak; confirm 429/503 triggers fire at thresholds and business writes are unaffected.\n3. Exercise a full drain cycle from a simulated outage; measure actual drain rate vs. the ~3,200/s estimate.\n4. Fill-test disk thresholds to confirm Level 2/3 triggers engage before exhaustion.\n\n### Still not costed or measured\nConsumer-host infrastructure for 6–7 replicas, EU object storage volume/egress, metadata-only PostgreSQL growth rate, and monitoring tooling — all within a EUR 900/month ceiling that **cannot be verified without quotes**."
    }
  },
