    "message": {
      "text": "## Consumer calculation (50% headroom)\n\nPer `capacity` against the measured 600 events/s per replica:\n\n| Scenario | Events/s | With 50% headroom | Consumers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New peak | 3,600 | 5,400 | **9** |\n| Average (unchanged) | 400 | 600 | 1 |\n\nThe decision record's nine-consumer target matches the tool result exactly: 3,600 × 1.5 = 5,400 ÷ 600 = 9. Average load still needs only 1 replica, so nine is peak provisioned capacity, not steady state.\n\n**Decision registered:** six-consumer target replaced by nine consumers, **conditional on validating database contention**. No Kafka adoption approved — the PostgreSQL outbox stays.\n\n## Revised rollout (launch 1 December 2026)\n\n1. **Contention validation (gating step, must complete before nine becomes final).** Nine consumers polling one PostgreSQL outbox is the stated risk. Load-test the outbox claim pattern (e.g., `SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`) with 9 concurrent pollers; measure lock waits, index bloat on the outbox table, and impact on the write transaction. Fallback if contention fails: batch-claim polling or a dispatcher/consumer split — staying on PostgreSQL, since Kafka is not approved.\n2. **Capacity staging.** Deploy 9 replicas; run steady state at 1–2 (average 400/s needs 1 with 50% headroom), autoscale or pre-scale to 9 for peak windows. Scale-out is stateless and cheap; replicas only claim outbox rows, so this is safe under at-least-once + event_id dedup.\n3. **Replay-window guardrails.** Nine consumers draining at up to 5,400/s clear backlogs fast; retain lag alerts (24h) and escalation (~3 days) against the 7-day raw retention window.\n4. **Storage check (unchanged conclusion).** If 3,600/s were sustained for a full day the tool returns 466.56 GB/day, but the historical peak profile is bursty (~20 min), so the 7-day ≈338 GiB estimate stands, plus ~5.4 GB per 20-minute peak episode at 3,600/s. The ~500 GiB expansion recommendation and EU-only constraint are unaffected.