      "text": "## Baseline re-read (historical measurements)\n\nFrom `inspect_system`: Go webhook receiver, PostgreSQL outbox in the same transaction, EU-only storage, 3 engineers, average 400 events/s, peak 2400 events/s for 20 min, 1500-byte payloads, 600 events/s sustained per consumer replica, 100 GiB free on existing PostgreSQL, EUR 900/month ceiling (no vendor quotes). This is treated as evidence about the past state, not as current decisions.\n\n## Storage computation (7-day retention, average load)\n\nPer `capacity` at 400 events/s, headroom 1 (constant rate, excluding indexes, replicas, compression, metadata):\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Raw bytes/day | 51,840,000,000 B = 51.84 GB ≈ 48.28 GiB |\n| **7 days raw payload** | 362,880,000,000 B = 362.88 GB ≈ **338 GiB** |\n| 30 days (superseded proposal) | 1,555.2 GB ≈ 1,448 GiB |\n| Reduction from change | ~76.7% less raw storage |\n\nA 20-minute peak episode adds only ~3.6 GB (2,000 extra events/s × 1,200 s × 1,500 B), so it doesn't move the total materially.\n\n## What this changes\n\n**Confirmed decisions (authoritative, per the change record):**\n- Launch: 1 December 2026 (replaces 15 November) — 16 days later.\n- Raw payload retention: 7 days (replaces 30-day proposal); metadata unchanged at 90 days.\n- Unchanged v1 decisions, now treated as confirmed: Go, PostgreSQL outbox, EU-only, EUR 900/month ceiling, at-least-once delivery with event_id deduplication.\n\n**Impacts on the plan:**\n\n1. **Storage is smaller but still doesn't fit.** 7-day retention cuts the requirement from ~1.45 TiB to ~338 GiB, but existing PostgreSQL has only 100 GiB free. The tool's figure excludes indexes, WAL, and metadata, so the real shortfall is larger than the nominal ~238 GiB gap. Storage work remains a launch blocker; it is now a smaller procurement that is easier to fit under the EUR 900/month ceiling.\n\n2. **Recovery and replay semantics shrink.** With at-least-once delivery and event_id dedup, raw payload retention was the implicit replay window. It is now 7 days: any downstream consumer that falls behind by more than 7 days cannot be replayed from raw payloads, and dead-lettered events needing reprocessing must be resolved within that window or archived separately. DR runbooks and consumer-lag alerting thresholds must be re-baselined to well under 7 days. Metadata at 90 days supports audit/lineage queries but not payload replay.\n\n3. **Timeline slip is absorbed by the retention cut.** The 16 extra days provide time for the storage expansion and the updated retention/replay runbooks without new scope.\n\n**Recommendations (not yet approved):**\n- Provision ~500 GiB additional EU-region storage, or move raw payloads to EU object storage with PostgreSQL holding metadata + pointers — likely cheaper within the EUR 900/month cap; vendor quotes still needed to confirm either option.\n- Add lag alerts at ~24h and a hard escalation at ~3 days so the 7-day replay window is never silently consumed.\n- Define a separate archive path for dead-lettered events that must outlive 7 days, since dedup means re-delivery alone won't recover them after expiry.\n- Note throughput sizing is unchanged: peak 2,400/s still needs 4 consumer replicas at 600/s each; average load needs 1.\n\n**Net effect:** the change record reduces storage cost and legal exposure while tightening the operational replay window, and the later launch date covers the remaining storage work. The core v1 architecture is untouched."
